<section style="padding:72px 24px 40px;"><div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;"><div class="rich"><p>Winnipeg installers advertise radon mitigation systems from about $1,500 to $3,000; a Regina installer quotes $2,500 to $3,500. Those are ranges individual companies publish, not a set price for either city, and not our quote. What your house costs depends on the house, and a certified radon professional will give you that number after looking at the basement.</p><p>Before any of that, you need a long-term test result. Health Canada's guideline is 200 Bq/m3 (becquerels per cubic metre, averaged over a year in the part of the home you actually live in), and a kit costs about $70 from the lung associations' HomeRadonTest.ca. Health Canada says radon is the number one cause of lung cancer in non-smokers, so the three months a proper test takes are worth it. If your result comes back high, the figures below are what to plan around.</p></div></div></section>
